Delivery standard (the hub judges by it whether the task is done): done = the change is live (deployed, released, or merged with automatic deployment) and you tested it in the real environment, with the readings attached to your final reply (the command and its output, a screenshot, the live version number or an endpoint's answer).
- Where the repository's and this computer's rules let you merge, deploy and enable it yourself, take it live and test it; do not stop at an open PR.
- What needs the user's go-ahead each time (merging in a repository that is not the user's, deploying to production, turning on a feature that is off by default, sending messages to others) is not yours to do: finish everything before it, and write in the reply what is to be approved and who does which step once it is.
- When stuck on CI, someone's review, a missing environment or permission, try yourself first (rerun once, another machine, an existing environment); when you cannot, write which gate it is stuck at and who can clear it.
- For a feature that is off by default, write every step to enable it; with only a PR open, or merged but not deployed, do not say "done".
- When nothing is to go live (research, a conclusion, local files only), none of this applies: report the result as usual.

End with the one structured closeout record below only, without a separate "Delivery:" line. delivery is live (live and tested), pr (stopped at a PR), merged (merged, not live), off (off by default, to be enabled) or none (nothing to go live); write the blocker in missing, who can clear it in unblock (user, agent or other) and what exactly is awaited in waiting. merged / deployed still state only what actually happened.